<h2>Devices With M6</h2><br/>
The &zwnj;Mac mini&zwnj; is the first Mac with an M6, but the chip is going to come to a couple of other products in the lineup.<br/>
<ul><br/>
 	<li><strong>MacBook Pro</strong> - The 14-inch entry-level <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/macbook-pro/">MacBook Pro</a> could be updated with an M6 chip in October 2026.</li><br/>
 	<li><strong>iMac</strong> - The 24-inch <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/imac/">iMac</a> is also expected to get the M6 chip, likely around the time the &zwnj;MacBook Pro&zwnj; is updated.</li><br/>
<li><strong>MacBook Air</strong> - The <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/macbook-air/">MacBook Air</a> could get the M6 chip in early 2027.</li><br/>
 	<li><strong>iPad Pro</strong> - The next <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/ipad-pro/">iPad Pro</a> could get the M6 chip.</li><br/>
 	<li><strong>iPad Air</strong> - A future version of the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/ipad-air/">iPad Air</a> is likely to adopt the M6. Apple usually keeps the &zwnj;iPad Air&zwnj; processor one generation behind the &zwnj;iPad Pro&zwnj;.</li><br/>
</ul><br/>
<h2>M6 Pro and M6 Max</h2><br/>
Apple <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/06/25/2027-macs-m7-chips/">isn't going to make</a> M6 Pro or M6 Max chips, according to <em>Bloomberg</em>, so the M6 will be the first standalone Apple silicon chip that won't have more powerful variants.<br/>
<br/>
Instead of manufacturing M6 Pro and M6 Max chips, Apple is speeding up its chip development timeline to bring the M7 Pro and M7 Max to market more quickly. The M7 Pro and M7 Max will have technologies that support on-device AI and GPU-intensive software, so they better meet growing demand for processors with those capabilities.<br/>
<h2>M5 Makes Up the Gap</h2><br/>
Macs that would have used an M6 Pro or M6 Max chip are going to be stuck with the M5 Pro and M5 Max until the M7 family is ready. That means the high-end <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/macbook-ultra/">OLED</a> "<a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/macbook-ultra/">MacBook Ultra</a>" we're expecting in late 2026 or early 2027 is going to use the same M5 Pro and M5 Max chips that are in the current &zwnj;MacBook Pro&zwnj; models. It's also possible it will get the M5 Ultra.<br/>
<br/>
The new &zwnj;Mac mini&zwnj; also got the M5 Pro instead of an M6 Pro chip. The new <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/mac-studio/">Mac Studio</a> announced alongside the &zwnj;Mac mini&zwnj; uses M5 Max and M5 Ultra chips.<br/>
<h2>Short Life Span</h2><br/>
The M6 isn't going to be around for long, because Apple plans to launch the M7 in the first half of 2027. The M7 Pro and M7 Max will follow at the end of the year.<br/>
<h2>M6 Features</h2><br/>
The M6 is Apple's first chip built on a new 2-nanometer process that increases transistor density on a smaller die. The process change improves performance and efficiency.<br/>
<br/>
It has a 12-core CPU with what Apple says is the world's fastest CPU core, a larger 12-core GPU with Neural Accelerators for each core, a new Dual 16-core Neural Engine, and up to 170GB/s memory bandwidth.<br/>
